Git & GitHub - Remote Repository Notes

## What is a Remote Repository?

A remote repository is a repository hosted on a server such as GitHub.

It allows us to store our code online and synchronize it with our local machine.

Example:

Local Repository <----> Remote Repository (GitHub)

---

## GitHub

GitHub is a service that provides hosting for Git repositories.

It allows developers to:
- Store projects online
- Collaborate with others
- Push local code to a remote repository
- Pull changes from a remote repository
- Track project history

---

# Important Git Commands

## 1. git clone

`git clone` is used to copy an existing remote repository from GitHub to our local machine.

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
Example:
git clone https://github.com/username/project.git
After cloning, a local copy of the GitHub repository is created.
Important Point
git clone copies the repository for the first time.
2. git push
git push is used to send our local commits to the remote repository.
git push
Example:
git add .
git commit -m "Added new program"
git push
Flow:
Local Repository | | git push ↓ Remote Repository (GitHub)
Meaning
git push = Upload local commits to the remote repository.
3. git pull
git pull is used to download the latest changes from the remote repository and update the local repository.
git pull
Example:
git pull origin main
Flow:
Remote Repository (GitHub) | | git pull ↓ Local Repository
Meaning
git pull = Get the latest changes from the remote repository.
Clone vs Pull vs Push
Command
Purpose
git clone
Copy a remote repository to your computer
git push
Send local commits to GitHub
git pull
Get changes from GitHub to your local repository
Working with a Remote Repository
A common workflow is:
git clone <repository-url>

# Make changes to the project

git add .

git commit -m "Added changes"

git push
If someone else has made changes to the remote repository:
git pull
Then continue working on the project.
Git Remote
A remote is a connection between our local Git repository and a remote repository.
The default remote name is usually:
origin
To see the remote repository:
git remote -v
Example output:
origin  https://github.com/username/project.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/username/project.git (push)
Branch and Remote Push
If we are working on a branch such as main, we can push it using:
git push origin main
Here:
git push → send commits
origin → remote repository
main → branch
